2.53.0

From: John Groves <John@Groves.net>

Add the famfs file ioctl handler (FAMFSIOC_NOP, FAMFSIOC_MAP_CREATE) and
the KABI-44 self-describing fmap message: the wire ABI in famfs_ioctl.h
(famfs_ioc_fmap_header plus the simple and interleaved extent structs), the
in-core famfs_file_meta, and famfs_file_init_dax(), which copies the
message in, parses both the simple-extent and interleaved (striped) wire
forms into inode->i_private, and sets S_DAX.

Resolving those mappings to dax-device offsets (iomap_begin) is added in
the following commit; the read/write/fault paths keep their NULL iomap_ops
stub until then.

Famfs now accepts extent and chunk alignment at either PMD or PTE. PMD
is the normal case, and it guarantees PMD faults - which is right for
large files, which are the original use case. But there are also active
use cases that use zillions of small files, and those need PTE support
to avoid big space amplification.

Also add famfs ioctls to ioctl-number.rst

Reviewed-by: Jeff Layton <jlayton@kernel.org>
Signed-off-by: John Groves <john@groves.net>
---
v14:
 - Require CAP_SYS_RAWIO in the MAP_CREATE handler (famfs_file_init_dax()).
   MAP_CREATE installs caller-supplied device-memory mappings into a file,
   so it now demands CAP_SYS_RAWIO in addition to the mount-wide
   FAMFS_OPT_MAP_CREATE policy gate (Richard Cheng).
 - Reject integer overflow while validating a caller-supplied fmap. Each
   extent's ext_offset + ext_len and the running extent_total are now
   accumulated with check_add_overflow(), and file_size (and each
   interleaved extent's ie_nbytes) is bounded by MAX_LFS_FILESIZE. Without
   this an overflowing extent could wrap a dax offset back toward 0 and
   resolve to the wrong memory (Richard Cheng; Darrick Wong).
 - Validate interleaved (striped) fmap coverage at ingest, so a malformed
   striped file is rejected with -EINVAL at MAP_CREATE rather than faulting
   (SIGBUS) on access. Two checks: (a) the sum of the interleaved extents'
   ie_nbytes must reach file_size (logical coverage); and (b) each strip
   must physically hold the chunks striped onto it -- checked by probing
   the highest file offset that lands on each strip (the last byte of each
   top-row chunk) and resolving it with the same arithmetic the fault path
   uses, which is partial-final-chunk aware and assumes nothing about
   relative strip sizes. Previously the check subtracted physical strip
   ext_len from file_size, conflating physical allocation with logical
   coverage (Richard Cheng). This is also the MAP_CREATE-time interleaved
   validation discussed with Darrick Wong on the opts patch.

